      "s65-c1-kinds",
      "Kinds of Stock",
      "Stocks can be grouped by how much income and growth they offer. The more a stock depends on growth, the riskier it usually is.\n• Defensive stocks come from companies that sell things people always need, like utilities, food, medicine, and household goods. They are the most conservative stocks. They grow slowly, swing less with the market, and often pay higher dividends.\n• Blue-chip stocks come from large, well-known, established companies. They offer some growth and pay steady dividends. They are also called large-cap stocks.\n• Value stocks look underpriced by the market. They carry more risk than defensive or blue-chip stocks but may earn more.\n• Growth stocks can rise faster than average, with more risk. High-tech and biotech companies are examples.\n• Mid-cap stocks are smaller than blue chips but may earn somewhat more.\n• Speculative stocks are small and very risky, such as penny stocks or stocks traded over the counter (OTC).\n\nMarket capitalization, or market cap, is the total market value of a company's shares:\n• Mega-cap: $200 billion or more\n• Large-cap: $10 billion to $200 billion\n• Mid-cap: $2 billion to $10 billion\n• Small-cap: $250 million to $2 billion\n• Micro-cap: less than $250 million",
